        A whole-food supplement from the wild Himalayan berry: rare Omega-7, natural Vitamin C, flavonoids and 190+ phytonutrients. A food, not a medicine — intended to support a nutrient-dense daily diet.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 p-4 sm:p-5 bg-[#02231A]/95 border-2 border-emerald-500/50 rounded-2xl shadow-2xl relative overflow-hidden backdrop-blur-sm">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">
          <span class="bg-amber-400 text-black text-[10px] font-black uppercase px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full tracking-wide">Direct Answer</span>
          <span class="text-xs text-emerald-300 font-semibold">Nutrient-Function Framing</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-200 leading-relaxed font-sans">
          <strong class="text-amber-300 font-bold">Why is Himalayan sea buckthorn a unique daily food supplement?</strong>
          Sujata Himalayan Sea Buckthorn delivers <strong class="text-emerald-300">rare Omega-7 (palmitoleic acid)</strong>, <strong class="text-emerald-300">Omega-3, 6 and 9</strong>, natural <strong class="text-emerald-300">Vitamin C</strong> and <strong class="text-emerald-300">190+ phytonutrients</strong> from wild Leh-Ladakh berries — nutrients that support a balanced, plant-forward diet. Typical adult serving: 1-2 capsules (650-1,300 mg) with food. This is a food supplement; it is not intended to diagnose, treat, cure or prevent any disease.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="bg-[#050B08] text-gray-200 py-12 px-4 sm:px-6 font-sans">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto space-y-12">

      <!-- 1. Why Omega-7 as a dietary nutrient -->
      <article class="bg-[#0A1A12] border border-emerald-900/50 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-xl">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-serif font-bold text-white mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-amber-400">1.</span> Omega-7: A Rare Plant-Food Nutrient
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Most Indian plates deliver Omega-6 easily (vegetable oils) and some Omega-3 (flaxseed, walnut). Omega-7 — palmitoleic acid — is far less common in everyday foods and is found in meaningful amounts in only a handful of plant sources. Sea buckthorn is one of them. Sujata harvests from wild Leh-Ladakh berries and cold-presses below 40 °C to keep these delicate fatty acids intact.
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 text-sm">
          <div class="bg-black/30 border border-emerald-500/20 rounded-xl p-4">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-emerald-400 text-base mb-2">① Omega-7 Fatty Acid</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed text-xs sm:text-sm">Palmitoleic acid is the signature nutrient of the sea-buckthorn berry — rarely present in the typical Indian diet.</p>
